Section: Helpers

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "True" else "False"

  beq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  beq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else beq-loop a b 0 (list-length a)

  beq-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  beq-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else beq-loop a b (i + 1) n

  the-key : Maybe X509Key = x509-mk-key KeyEd25519 v-pub

Section: The Signed Content

  r-content-len : Text = "cv content-len=" & show (list-length (tls-cv-content True v-transcript))

 The decisive structural line: our content is byte-for-byte what the spec
 says, including an ASCII context string.

  r-content-exact : Text =
    "cv content-matches-spec=" & yn (beq (tls-cv-content True v-transcript) v-expected-content)

 The two roles must produce DIFFERENT bytes. If they do not, a server's
 signature can be reflected back at a client as if the client had made it.

  r-roles-differ : Text =
    "cv roles-differ=" & yn (beq (tls-cv-content True v-transcript) (tls-cv-content False v-transcript) == False)

Section: Sign And Verify

  server-cv : List Integer = tls-cv-encode True v-priv v-pub v-transcript

  truncated-cv : List Integer = tlsc-slice server-cv 0 40

  v-other-transcript : List Integer = [9]

  cv-check : Boolean, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  cv-check (expect-server) (msg) (th) =
    when the-key
      is None -> False
      is Just (k) -> tls-cv-verify expect-server k msg th

  r-cv-len : Text = "cv msg-len=" & show (list-length server-cv)

  r-cv-good : Text = "cv verify-server=" & yn (cv-check True server-cv v-transcript)

 The cross-role check, and the reason the context strings exist. A verifier
 EXPECTING a client must reject a signature a server made -- even though the
 signature is perfectly valid, made by the right key, over the right
 transcript. The role is bound INTO the signature, not asserted alongside
 it.

  r-cv-reflected : Text = "cv verify-as-client=" & yn (cv-check False server-cv v-transcript)

 A different transcript is a different signature. This is what stops a man
 in the middle who swaps a key share: the transcript he produced is not the
 one the real peer ever signed.

  r-cv-othertx : Text = "cv verify-wrong-transcript=" & yn (cv-check True server-cv v-other-transcript)

  r-cv-trunc : Text = "cv verify-truncated=" & yn (cv-check True truncated-cv v-transcript)

Section: Certificate Message

  cert-list : List (List Integer) = [v-cert]

  cert-msg : List Integer = tls-cert-encode cert-list

  r-msg-len : Text = "cert msg-len=" & show (list-length cert-msg)

  parse-ok : List Integer -> Boolean
  parse-ok (msg) =
    when tls-cert-parse msg
      is None -> False
      is Just (cs) -> one-cert cs

  one-cert : List (List Integer) -> Boolean
  one-cert (cs) =
    if list-length cs /= 1 then False
    else beq (list-at cs 0) v-cert

  r-roundtrip : Text = "cert roundtrip=" & yn (parse-ok cert-msg)

 A Certificate message is the first thing an UNAUTHENTICATED peer sends us,
 so every length in it is hostile until checked.

  parse-rejects : List Integer -> Boolean
  parse-rejects (msg) =
    when tls-cert-parse msg
      is None -> True
      is Just (cs) -> False

  r-bad-len : Text = "cert rej-truncated=" & yn (parse-rejects (tlsc-slice cert-msg 0 20))

  r-empty : Text = "cert rej-empty=" & yn (parse-rejects [])

Section: signature_algorithms

  r-sigalgs : Text = "ext sigalgs-len=" & show (list-length tls-ext-sig-algs)

  sig-at : Integer -> Integer
  sig-at (i) = list-at tls-ext-sig-algs i * 256 + list-at tls-ext-sig-algs (i + 1)

  r-sigalgs-schemes : Text =
    "ext sigalgs-schemes=" & show (sig-at 6) & "," & show (sig-at 8) & "," & show (sig-at 10) & "," & show (sig-at 12)

  r-sigalgs-type : Text = "ext sigalgs-type=" & show (sig-at 0)

